-- DICT-SEMANTICS §6.1 choice-point 3: "Non-ground goals: SELECTION COMMITS AT
-- THE ELABORATION SITE (specialization is not retroactive)." The spec flags this
-- as the one place most-specific-wins is weaker than it looks, and says it
-- "states it loudly rather than letting it be discovered". This fixture is that
-- statement, executable.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived):
--   line 1, `wrap (1 : Int)`. Inside `wrap : Tag a => a -> Int` the goal is
--     `Tag (List a)` over a RIGID, signature-bound `a`. Only the general
--     instance matches it, and it is discharged THERE, ONCE, when `wrap` is
--     elaborated. "If `f` is later used at `a := Int`, `f`'s body still runs the
--     general instance's evidence (closed over the caller's `Default Int` dict);
--     it is NOT retroactively re-resolved." So the body runs `10 + tag y` with
--     `tag` the caller-supplied `Tag Int` dict: 10 + 1               => 11
--   line 2, `tag ([1] : List Int)`. A GROUND goal at the same predicate: the
--     matching set has `Tag (List Int)` as its ⊑-minimum               => 99
--
-- So the SAME ground predicate `Tag (List Int)` receives DIFFERENT evidence at
-- the two lines. §6.1.3 is explicit that this does not violate §6 coherence
-- (which quantifies over derivations of the SAME judgment) and that GHC behaves
-- identically -- "but it is real and observable".
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all ACCEPT and print 11 then 99. CONFORMANT.
--
-- ⚠️ DO NOT "FIX" LINE 1 TO 99. A reviewer who reads only line 2 will read 11 as
-- a most-specific-wins failure. It is the specified behaviour, and the
-- alternatives §6.1.3 lists are (i) rejecting the generic code that motivates
-- the general instance at all, or (ii) whole-program monomorphization, which §8
-- rules out. If the owner ever revisits this trade, this row is the one to move.
interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> Int

impl Tag Int where
  tag _ = 1

impl Tag (List a) requires Tag a where
  tag xs = match xs
    (y :: _) => 10 + tag y
    [] => 10

impl Tag (List Int) where
  tag _ = 99

wrap : Tag a => a -> Int
wrap x = tag [x]

main =
  println (wrap (1 : Int))
  println (tag ([1] : List Int))
